---
/**
 * `<Bench>` — one instrument and the prose that explains it, side by side
 * (redesign 2026-08, amendment L-2).
 *
 * WHAT PROBLEM THIS SOLVES. Until now a lesson was an essay with a figure in it:
 * the drawing sat in the reading column at 824px, the sentence describing what
 * to press sat *below* the thing it described, and the run's ledger was folded
 * shut under both. A reader following "watch `lo` and `hi` close in" had to hold
 * the sentence in memory, scroll, and look. That is textbook split attention, on
 * the one page whose entire promise is that you can WATCH the algorithm.
 *
 * A bench pairs exactly one instrument with the prose that narrates it. On a wide
 * screen the prose scrolls in the reading column while the instrument stays
 * pinned beside it, so the sentence and the thing it describes are on screen at
 * the same time — and `<StepLink>` in that prose now moves a drawing the reader
 * can see, rather than scrolling them away from the sentence they were reading.
 *
 * DOM ORDER IS THE MOBILE ORDER, and it is deliberately unchanged from what the
 * lesson shipped before: heading, prose, instrument. The two columns are a grid
 * placement at ≥1200px, never a reordering — so a narrow screen, a printed page,
 * and a screen reader all get the sequence the prose was written for. Nothing is
 * hoisted.
 *
 * WHY STICKY WORKS HERE. The stage is a grid item with `align-items: start`, so
 * it keeps its own content height while its containing block is as tall as the
 * reading column. That difference IS the travel. A bench whose prose cannot
 * outrun its instrument therefore has nothing to stick to — which is what `solo`
 * is for.
 *
 * Props:
 * - `solo`  the prose here is too short to give the pin any travel (a bare
 *           heading, or a couple of challenge cards). The bench renders as one
 *           full-width column instead, which is *wider* than the old reading
 *           column rather than narrower — a comparison set of three sorts reads
 *           better stacked at full width than squeezed into half of one.
 *           Authored, not inferred: only the author knows whether the prose
 *           beside an instrument is its explanation or just a label.
 * - `id`    optional anchor, for the lesson's `#visualizer` contract.
 *
 * There is no `<noscript>` kill-switch and no client script: a bench is CSS
 * layout over content that already works, so with JavaScript off it is simply a
 * lesson section in its original order.
 */
interface Props {
  solo?: boolean;
  id?: string;
}

const { solo = false, id } = Astro.props;
---

<div class:list={['bench', solo && 'bench--solo']} id={id}>
  <div class="bench__read"><slot /></div>
  <div class="bench__stage"><slot name="stage" /></div>
</div>

<style>
  /* Below the pinning breakpoint a bench is exactly what the lesson always was:
     two blocks in source order, prose then instrument. Every rule that makes it
     two columns lives inside the media query, so there is one code path for
     phones, print and forced-colors and it is the untouched one. */
  .bench {
    display: block;
  }

  .bench__stage {
    margin-block-start: var(--space-6);
  }

  /* The stage is a lesson-body child at mobile widths, where LessonLayout caps
     prose at --measure. It must break that cap: the drawing is not prose. */
  .bench__read,
  .bench__stage {
    min-width: 0;
  }

  /* 1200px is where a --stage-w pane and a full reading measure both fit without
     either being squeezed; min-height keeps a pinned pane off short viewports,
     where sticking something 600px tall leaves the reader nothing to scroll. */
  @media (min-width: 1200px) and (min-height: 640px) {
    .bench:not(.bench--solo) {
      display: grid;
      grid-template-columns: minmax(0, 1fr) var(--stage-w);
      column-gap: var(--space-10);
      /* NOT `stretch`: a stretched grid item is already as tall as its row, so
         `position: sticky` on it would have zero travel. Shrinking the stage to
         its content is what gives the pin somewhere to go. */
      align-items: start;
    }

    .bench:not(.bench--solo) .bench__stage {
      position: sticky;
      /* The same offset every sticky thing on the site derives (--header-h), plus
         one space unit so the pane is not welded to the keyline. */
      top: calc(var(--header-h) + var(--space-4));
      margin-block-start: 0;
      /* A tall instrument (the graph, the BST at depth) must not push its own
         controls off the bottom of the viewport. Capping the PANE — not the
         canvas — keeps `measureCanvas`'s horizontal-overflow rule untouched, so
         the a11y contract that decides the canvas's tabindex is unaffected. */
      max-height: calc(100dvh - var(--header-h) - var(--space-8));
      /* MEASURED: binary search's instrument is 932px of drawing, legend,
         sentence, transport, input disclosure and a four-row table; a 1280x900
         laptop leaves it 804. The instrument is simply richer than a short
         viewport, so the pane keeps a scroll of its own as the safety net — the
         part that falls below the fold is the tail of the ledger, which is the
         one region a reader chooses to look at rather than one they need in
         view. `contain` stops a flick that reaches the end of the table from
         carrying the whole page with it. Everything above the table — the
         drawing, the sentence and the transport — fits at every height. */
      overflow-y: auto;
      overscroll-behavior: contain;
      /* Room for the focus ring of whatever control sits at the pane's edge;
         without it the ring is clipped by the scroll container. */
      padding-inline: var(--space-1);
      margin-inline: calc(var(--space-1) * -1);
      scrollbar-gutter: stable;
    }
  }

  /* Print gets the source order at full width; a pinned pane means nothing on
     paper, and `position: sticky` in a paged context is undefined behaviour. */
  @media print {
    .bench {
      display: block;
    }

    .bench__stage {
      position: static;
      max-height: none;
      overflow: visible;
    }
  }
</style>
